course: dictionary
lesson: 8769
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Sewer is one spelling with two very different meanings: a large underground pipe, and a person who sews. The words around it, the context, tell you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «Sewers are big underground tunnels that carry dirty water and rain to treatment works.» The clues are underground, dirty water and treatment works, so sewer means the pipe. Try the other meaning in the sentence: a person who sews does not carry dirty water, so it cannot be right.
explain: If someone is sewing a button on your coat, that person is a sewer. The clues are sewing, button and coat. Always ask which meaning fits the clues around the word, and check by putting your meaning back into the sentence.
fact: A sewer (meaning 1) is a large underground pipe or tunnel that carries away dirty water and waste from toilets, sinks and streets.
fact: A sewer (meaning 2) is a person who sews. It is said «SOH-er».
fact: «London's main sewers were built in the 1860s by Joseph Bazalgette and helped end the city's outbreaks of cholera.»
fact: «Fat poured down the sink sets hard in sewers and, with wet wipes, makes giant lumps called fatbergs.»
fact: Words spelled the same but with different meanings and sounds are called heteronyms. Sewer is one of them.
exercise: «Sewers are big underground tunnels that carry dirty water and rain to treatment works.» What is a sewer in this sentence? | A kind of tunnel for trains | A person who sews clothes | *An underground pipe or tunnel that carries dirty water | A pump that brings clean water
exercise: Mum is a sewer; she mends our school uniform with a needle. What does sewer mean here? | A rat that lives in drains | An underground pipe | *A person who sews | A dark tunnel under the streets
exercise: «Brown rats live wherever people live; they eat our waste and shelter in drains, sewers and buildings.» In this sentence, what are sewers? | A park bench | *Underground places where waste goes | A kind of warm clothing | A sewing shop
test: Which sentence uses sewer to mean a person who sews? | The sewer carried dirty water to the works | The rats lived in the sewer under the street | *The sewer stitched the torn jacket with a needle and thread | The sewer was built in London in the 1860s
test: How is sewer said when it means a person who sews? | *SOH-er | Like the word sure | Like the word sour | Like the word soup
exam: Jayden finds the word sewer and cannot tell which meaning to use. What should he do first? | Decide it can only ever mean a pipe | *Read the words around it and check which meaning fits the sentence | Pick the first meaning he remembers without checking | Skip the sentence and never come back to it
